A||||8||rnoswal||Just what is the oiling problem with a 2.8?||||1||
Z||000000||rnoswal||04-24-2001||10:57 PM||||I have a 2.8 that I eventually want to build up to take the place of the 4 cyl. I keep hearing about oiling problems with the 2.8. What are they and is there anything that can be done about it to help it out some or alot? I am going to be putting a Holley 500 carb on it and trying to make it stronger than stock. Any suggestions on the oiling would help.<P>Thanks<BR>Russ||||reg||1
Z||000001||Fiero_Freak||04-24-2001||11:43 PM||||   They only problem per ser that I've heard of is main bearings.  I read somewhere about some that had groves to flow more oil?  <P>   Has anyone heard about those?||||reg||5
Z||000002||TheFiero64||04-25-2001||05:04 PM||||I think the 2.8 block has bad flow to the mains and can spin bearings at high RPM. I would put in a high volume pump if you plan on playing near the <B>redline</B><P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://people.ne.mediaone.net/fiero64/Fiero642.jpg"> <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||3
Z||000003||Dave Gunsul||04-25-2001||08:04 PM||||Use a high vol. oil pump, windage try,quality<BR>bearings, and a 52 oil filter.<BR>BTW the V6's oiling prob. is greatly exaggerated. A proper break in period followed by changing the oil at 3,000 mile intervals will make your engine last a long time.<BR>GM makes an alum. V6 block that has a revised<BR>oiling system to deal with all out racing if<BR>you're really concerned. It'll cost ya though.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
Z||000004||JR||04-25-2001||09:50 PM||||Well Dave, the oiling system issue is not to be taken lightly for anyone thinking of driving their 2.8 aggressively.  Repeated runs close to redline in 1985 and 1986 Fieros is tempting fate.  1987 and 1988 have lead-in grooves in the manin-bearing journals make them much more robust.  Why did GM make this change?  Other mods such as cross-drilling the two centre main-bearings to increase oil flow to the connecting rod bearings is usually the reason to install a high pressure oil pump.  <BR>Yes, change oil frequiently.  I use synthetic; when new it is so clear that I have difficulty reading it on the dipstick, about a month later finally enough deposits to register a golden color on the dipstick. That's when I change it.  Expensive, yes, but the engine mods cost me 10,000 dollars.<P>------------------<BR>85GT (daily driver 120,000 miles)<BR>88GT (highly modified sunday driver)||||reg||1
Z||000005||rnoswal||04-25-2001||10:50 PM||||Hey JR, what kind of mods do you have on your engine and is it a 2.8 or something else?<P>Thanks<BR>Russ||||reg||1
Z||000006||GT GT 87||04-25-2001||11:27 PM||||I took apart my '86 gt motor and it had the lead in grooves in the main bearing to help the ROD bearings!  Shaun sold me his preped '85 GT block and it had the same lead in groves in the block ..  What ever mods you do  Never use a PF-47 ,,  Allways the PF-52 !!!  I have used only Mobil One in the 3 GT's I have owned and it really keeps the inside of the engine clean..........   Steve||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fierowrecker||04-26-2001||09:53 PM||||Go to a 3.8L if you want performance...<BR>If you want stock, go 2.5L...<BR>crash...||||reg||1
Z||000008||Dave Gunsul||04-26-2001||10:15 PM||||JR I didn't say to take the oil system lightly as a matter of fact I listed several<BR>improvments to make sure that the engine will<BR>last a long time. What I ment was; people act<BR>like the 2.8 will blow up no matter whats been done to it. I think that a good break in period is the key to making the engine last a long time, seems to me that a lot of<BR>people start beating on new engines within<BR>a 1,000 miles of replacing it! Try putting<BR>on a nice 5,000 baby ;-) miles before going<BR>out to a trak and beating the crap out of it.<P>I'm not trying to start an argument here these are just my opinions based on my own personal experience with my own cars and my<BR>friends cars, your mileage may vary. ;-)<BR>;-)<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
